+// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+// Memory-model audit for `mi_page_s` atomics (B2, 10-point summary):
+//  (1) `page->free` is a non-atomic pointer, mutated ONLY under ownership (the `xthread_free`
+//      owned bit is the ownership proof); concurrent readers use `mi_atomic_load_relaxed` --
+//      a torn read cannot occur on the supported targets (pointer-sized, naturally aligned)
+//      and any stale value is only CAS fodder / a hint (re-checked on the owner path). Never
+//      dereference a relaxed-read `page->free` without ownership.
+//  (2) `page->xthread_free`: relaxed load + acq_rel CAS push (see `mi_free_block_mt`) -- the
+//      release part publishes the block + its `next`; the acquire part pairs with pushes.
+//  (3) `page->xthread_free`: acq_rel CAS/exchange collect (see `mi_page_thread_free_collect`)
+//      -- acquire materializes the chain; release publishes the removal. `release` alone is
+//      NOT enough (answers the historical `release is enough?` question in `page.c`).
+//  (4) Ownership hand-off (`mi_abandoned_page_unown_from_free`): strong acq_rel CAS (see the
+//      local comment in `free.c`); strong, not weak: each failure must refresh `tf_expect`.
+//  (5) `page->xthread_id` (+flags in the low bits): relaxed loads classify in the `mi_free`
+//      fast path; flag updates are CAS'd acq_rel. The relaxed read is a hint -- everything it
+//      selects is validated afterwards (padding/ownership re-checks).
+//  (6) `xused` (packed `_Atomic`, relaxed RMW): owner-only traffic (owned bit = single
+//      writer), so relaxed suffices; it is `_Atomic` only because the 32-bit packed slot
+//      would tear against `used_free` under plain 64-bit RMW on ILP32.
+//  (7) `local_free`: owner-only, non-atomic (ownership invariant covers it).
+//  (8) Queue links `next`/`prev`: mutated under ownership + the page-queue lock; no lock-free
+//      concurrent mutation exists.
+//  (9) `memid` and other const provenance: written before the page is published into a queue
+//      (publication = release store of the queue link), so plain reads after publication are
+//      ordered.
+//  (10) Rules adopted from these cases: (a) never loosen a memory order purely for
+//      performance -- the B2 acquire->relaxed relaxation applies only to CAS-fodder loads
+//      (items 1/2); (b) never use release-only where the consumer must *read* the published
+//      data (acquire is required there); (c) relaxed is reserved for counters/hints whose
+//      consumers perform no cross-thread data-dependent reads.
